  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1030 PM CDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nThe current surface analysis shows high pressure stretching from \nthe northern Plains across the western Great Lakes, delivering \nclear to partly cloudy skies and light winds across northern \nWisconsin. On the western flank of the ridge, a warm front is \nadvancing northward across the central Plains. High pressure will\nbring quiet weather through Friday afternoon before warm \nadvection surges into the state. Unseasonably warm and breezy \nconditions will dominate the region over the weekend before a cold\nfront arrives late Sunday night. \n\nPatchy Frost Early Friday Morning: High pressure centered \nover Wisconsin tonight will create favorable radiational cooling \nconditions beneath light winds and partial sky clearing. Overnight\nminimum temperatures will drop into the mid to upper 30s across \nnorth-central and far northeast Wisconsin, including Land O Lakes,\nRhinelander, and Crandon. NBM statistical guidance shows a 50 to \n80 percent probability of temperatures falling below 37 degrees, \nsupporting patchy frost development. Rapid diurnal solar heating \nafter 13Z Friday will burn off patchy frost with daytime highs \nrecovering into the upper 60s under mostly sunny skies. \n\nUnseasonable Weekend Heat And Marine Hazards: As surface high \npressure shifts to the East Coast, an intense upper-level ridge \nwill build across the western Great Lakes, increasing warm \nadvection this weekend. Inland daytime high temperatures on \nSaturday and Sunday will climb into the upper 70s to mid-80s \nacross Wisconsin, putting daily record high values in jeopardy. \nTightening regional pressure gradients will drive gusty southerly \nwinds of 25 to 35 mph, while NBM probabilistic guidance shows a 60\nto 90 percent probability of wind gusts exceeding 30 mph.\n\nEarly Week Cold Front Shower Probabilities: A cold front \nassociated with a northern stream shortwave will cross Wisconsin \nlate Sunday night into Monday, bringing rain shower chances. \nIngestion of Gulf moisture remains limited, keeping 24-hour \nprecipitation probabilities between 30 and 50 percent across \ncentral and northern Wisconsin. NBM probabilistic guidance \nindicates only a 15 to 25 percent probability of 24-hour liquid \naccumulations exceeding 0.25 inches, reflecting a light rainfall \nevent. Post-frontal cold air advection Monday night will drop \ndaytime high temperatures back into the upper 50s and lower 60s \nfor early next week. \n\nMidweek System Rain Shower Potential: Another mid-level shortwave\ntrough and associated surface wave will traverse the Upper \nMidwest Wednesday into Thursday, increasing precipitation chances \nacross the forecast area once again. Dynamic forcing and renewed \nmoisture transport will support a 40 to 50 percent chance of rain \nshowers area-wide. Elevated instability up to 400 J/kg may allow \nfor isolated thunderstorm activity, though severe weather \nchances remain near zero. NBM ensemble guidance exhibits tight \ntemperature spreads with daytime highs staying held in the upper \n50s and lower 60s under mostly cloudy skies. A significant cooling\ntrend will follow this system for the end of the week.",
